# Review package: batch 6, slice mode

## 3cbef0dc Run the app in the mode the keeper gives it: lead, read, maintenance or off

The app reads keeper.json from the control folder every second (AppMode), and
an onRequest gate refuses what the mode does not allow: reads pass in read and
maintenance, everything but GET /api/health is refused while off, and a
websocket upgrade is refused outside lead. Work only a leader does (update
check, link sync, the minute timers and their start-up sweeps, which now
include checkExternalWaits) registers with whenLead; node messages go through
whileLeading, so a message queued when the app leaves lead writes nothing.
Leaving lead closes open sockets with 1012, and the session hand-over to the
link's cookie runs only in lead. Health answers 503 while off and carries the
mode outside the link. Without HORNHOOF_KEEPER the app leads from onReady as
before. Proved by test/app-mode.test.ts, version, health, config and socket
tests.

Ruling: NodeHub's onMessage wrapped is receiveNodeMessage, not handleNodeMessage - why: the plan was written before lifecycle.ts took the node.stopping message - cost if wrong: none, the wrapper sits on the same seam
Ruling: AppMode.settled() added and awaited by onReady - why: the old onReady awaited links.sync, and tests rely on the app being ready after it - cost if wrong: one small public method to remove
Ruling: stop() moves the mode to off - why: the leave hooks must run before the database closes, and a closed app should refuse work - cost if wrong: requests during close get 503 starting
Ruling: the full server suite was not run - why: the brief forbids a writer from running it, the author check runs the related tests - cost if wrong: a lead-work regression shows at the batch check

